---
import SiteLayout from '../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import SignalWalkthrough from '../../components/SignalWalkthrough.astro';
import '../../styles/exploration.css';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="When is a signal too old? — Fidret"
  description="Explore a synthetic signal-freshness experiment: source age, exact expiry, delayed observations and two-observation recovery."
  canonical="https://fidret.com/explorations/signal-freshness/"
  variant="lab"
>
  <main id="main" class="exploration-page">
    <header class="exploration-intro shell">
      <a class="exploration-back" href="/lab/">Fidret Lab</a>
      <div class="exploration-heading-grid">
        <h1>A reading can arrive.<br />And still be too late.</h1>
        <div class="exploration-lead">
          <p>When does an observation stop being useful? Explore what happens when a signal falls silent, arrives late, and returns.</p>
          <p class="exploration-scope">A synthetic software walkthrough. Fixed snapshots, invented data, no hardware.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="exploration-bench-section shell" aria-labelledby="bench-title">
      <div class="exploration-bench-intro">
        <h2 id="bench-title">The signal, under interruption.</h2>
        <p>One clock. A three-second age limit. Two distinct fresh observations to recover.</p>
      </div>
      <SignalWalkthrough />
    </section>

    <section class="exploration-reading shell" aria-labelledby="reading-title">
      <div class="exploration-reading-heading">
        <h2 id="reading-title">A signal needs<br />an explicit deadline.</h2>
        <p>This experiment asks a narrow question: should the latest accepted reading be exposed as current?</p>
      </div>
      <div class="exploration-rule-list">
        <article>
          <h3>Measure from observation.</h3>
          <p>A packet’s arrival time does not make its contents new. The model compares the current time with the source timestamp.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Make the boundary exact.</h3>
          <p>Age must be less than three seconds. At exactly three seconds, the reading is stale and the current value is withheld.</p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Give recovery its own conditions.</h3>
          <p>The first fresh observation begins recovery. A second, strictly newer one completes it. A duplicate or older timestamp cannot count again.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="exploration-comparison" aria-labelledby="comparison-title">
      <div class="shell exploration-comparison-grid">
        <div>
          <h2 id="comparison-title">What a last-arrival register misses.</h2>
          <p>The experiment also contains a deliberately simple comparison: keep the latest packet and offer it indefinitely.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="exploration-comparison-detail">
          <p>At 4 s, that register still offers <strong>21</strong>, even though its source observation has expired. At 5 s, it replaces that value with <strong>22</strong> from the already-stale packet.</p>
          <p>The freshness model withholds both. That demonstrates this software rule under these inputs. It does not establish that withholding a value is the right physical response.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="exploration-notes shell" aria-labelledby="notes-title">
      <div>
        <h2 id="notes-title">Keep the limits with the result.</h2>
        <p>A timely number can still be wrong. This walkthrough is an invitation to inspect a policy, not a validated design for operating equipment.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="exploration-disclosures">
        <details open>
          <summary>What this model assumes</summary>
          <p>Source timestamps and receiver time share one synchronized, synthetic monotonic clock. Values are finite scalars. Three seconds and two observations are illustrative choices, not validated operating requirements.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What the interface does</summary>
          <p>Each choice displays a fixed, tested snapshot from one sequence. Nothing advances in real time. The model requires a new time check before using a value later; an earlier snapshot does not update itself.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What remains outside the experiment</summary>
          <p>No clock drift, real transport, sensor calibration, identity, authentication, equipment, actuation or safety assessment is modeled. Clock resets require a separately designed policy. Representative evaluation and a responsible operator would be separate work.</p>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How this relates to the Lab</summary>
          <p>This walkthrough complements our written Lab pieces. It presents one fixed sequence from the underlying synthetic software model.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</SiteLayout>
